Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: enable true inline rotation on SM8550 and SM8650

The SSPP true inline rotator is present on both DPU 9.0 (SM8550/QCS8550) and
DPU 10.0 (SM8650) as revision v2.0.1, but the catalog never declares it, so
every VIG pipe on these SoCs reports DRM_MODE_ROTATE_0 plus the reflect bits
and nothing else. A portrait-mounted panel driven by a landscape compositor
therefore has to be rotated in a full-screen GPU composite pass on every single
frame -- no direct scanout, no partial composition, no cursor plane -- which is
the most expensive way to put a pixel on a 60 Hz handheld panel.

Declare the rotator on the four VIG pipes of both catalogs and give it a
format list. v2.0.1 rotates packed RGB as well as NV12/P010, so list the
8888 and 2101010 variants alongside them; XRGB2101010 in particular is what
a compositor asking for a 10-bit scanout surface will hand us.

Three things had to be fixed before the path could work at all. None of them
can ever have been exercised, because the only SoC that declared a rotator
until now (SC7280) lists NV12 as its sole rotatable format and has no
portrait panel in tree:

  - The pre-rotation height check read the source WIDTH while its own debug
    message printed a height (and printed src.y2, an absolute coordinate,
    rather than the extent). rot_maxheight bounds the number of source lines
    the rotator's line buffer has to hold, i.e. the height. As written the
    check rejected every landscape source wider than 1088 -- which is every
    realistic one.

  - The pipe reservation in dpu_rm.c tested `features & DPU_SSPP_INLINE_ROTATION`.
    That constant is an enum INDEX, not a mask, so the test was meaningless:
    it would happily hand out a rotation request to a pipe with no rotator, and
    reject one that had it, depending on unrelated feature bits. Use test_bit()
    like every other reader of that field.

  - DRM_MODE_ROTATE_90 is defined counter-clockwise; the DPU inline rotator
    turns the source clockwise. The two differ by a half turn, so fold
    REFLECT_X | REFLECT_Y into the reflect bits the hardware already applies.

Finally, keep QSEED programmed when rotating even for a 1:1 RGB pipe. The
rotator output is fed through the scaler, so the scaler cannot be left
disabled just because the source and destination extents match.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, and it is load-bearing for anyone reading a black
panel: the plane's rotation property is a static capability, but
dpu_plane_check_inline_rotation() is a per-frame test with two hard
requirements that the property cannot express.

  1. The framebuffer must be UBWC-compressed. A linear scanout buffer is
     rejected, every frame.
  2. The pre-rotation source height must be <= 1088 lines. A 1920x1080
     landscape source fits. A 2560x1440 one does not, and never will.

So a compositor that reads DRM_MODE_ROTATE_90 off the primary plane, stops
rotating in its own composite step and commits a rotated plane will get
-EINVAL on every flip on a 1440p panel, or on any panel if its buffers are
linear. That is a black screen, not a slow one. The compositor-side escape
hatch is to force composite rotation on those configurations.

Authored by tiopex <tiopxyz@gmail.com> as two per-SoC patches. Adapted here
into one, because novadeck builds a single Image for every board: the SM8550
and SM8650 halves share all of their dpu_plane.c, dpu_rm.c and
dpu_hw_catalog.c changes, so the shared hunks are carried once and the two
rotation configs (identical -- both v2.0.1, both 1088 lines) are collapsed
into one.

NOT UPSTREAM as of the 7.2.5 tree this is pinned to: neither DPU 9.0 nor
10.0 declares a rotator there, and both bugs above are present in it.

HW-VALIDATED 2026-09-12 on three boards from one dev card, both catalog
entries exercised:
  Pocket ACE  qcs8550  DPU 9.0   1080x1620  PASS  rotation=8, 0 flip errors
  Pocket FIT  sm8650   DPU 10.0  1080x1920  PASS  rotation=8, 0 flip errors
  Pocket S2   sm8650   DPU 10.0  1440x2560  rejected at the 1088 cap, by design
Orientation, colour and absence of tearing confirmed by eye on both passing
boards. Note what the PASS actually proves about the rotation direction:
gamescope asks for ROTATE_270, drm_rotation_simplify() rewrites that to
ROTATE_90 | REFLECT_X | REFLECT_Y since only ROTATE_90 is advertised, and the
XOR above cancels both reflects back to a bare ROT90. A correct image is
therefore a direct test of the CCW/CW correction -- without it the panel would
come up upside down.

What it costs to NOT have this, measured on Pocket ACE with an idle Steam UI,
interleaved A/B, 30 s windows, at matched present counts (878 vs 881 display
IRQs):
  scanout rotation (this patch)   gamescope GPU busy 0 ms/30 s   2.896 W
  composite rotation (before)     gamescope GPU busy 2205 ms/30 s 3.098 W
i.e. rotating in the GPU composite burns ~7.4% of the GPU continuously and
~0.2 W, and the display controller does it for free. Two scanout runs agreed
to within 7 mW, so the gap is ~29x the run-to-run spread. That is the IDLE
figure; the composite pass is per presented frame, so a game should widen it.

The compositor-side escape hatch for a panel over the cap is wired up in
rootfs/overlay/etc/novadeck/session.conf, keyed on the panel's native width.

